{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"capacity"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","text":"Int","identifier":"doc:\/\/com.apple.Swift\/documentation\/Swift\/Int"},{"kind":"text","text":" { "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"get"},{"kind":"text","text":" }"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"discussion","level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every array reserves a specific amount of memory to hold its contents."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"When you add elements to an array and that array begins to exceed its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"reserved capacity, the array allocates a larger region of memory and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"copies its elements into the new storage. The new storage is a multiple"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of the old storage’s size. This exponential growth strategy means that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"appending an element happens in constant time, averaging the performance"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of many append operations. Append operations that trigger reallocation"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"have a performance cost, but they occur less and less often as the array"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"grows larger."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The following example creates an array of integers from an array literal,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"then appends the elements of another collection. Before appending, the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"array allocates new storage that is large enough store the resulting"},{"